// Mastering Go - interactive notes data, part 3 (chapters 7-8)
window.DATA.chapters.push(
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------
  {
    id: `ch7`, part: 3, number: 7,
    title: `Telling a UNIX System What to Do`,
    overview: `<p>Systems programming in Go: talking to the operating system through files, processes, configuration and the command line. Because Go services mostly run in Linux containers, the chapter is written with UNIX in mind, but almost everything is portable. It starts with <strong>standard input, output and error</strong> and the difference between programs, processes and threads, then builds file I/O on two tiny interfaces, <strong>io.Reader</strong> and <strong>io.Writer</strong>, and shows how <strong>bufio</strong> buffering cuts system calls.</p><p>You read text files <strong>line by line, word by word and character by character</strong>, read raw bytes from <code>/dev/random</code> with <code>encoding/binary</code>, read fixed-size chunks and write files four different ways. Then come <strong>JSON</strong> (<code>Marshal</code>/<code>Unmarshal</code>, struct tags such as <code>omitempty</code> and <code>-</code>, streaming encoders and decoders, pretty printing), professional CLIs with <strong>viper</strong> (flags, environment variables, JSON config files) and <strong>cobra</strong> (commands, subcommands, aliases, persistent vs local flags), the Go 1.16 additions (<strong>embed</strong>, <code>os.ReadDir</code>/<code>DirEntry</code>, <strong>io/fs</strong>, the deprecation of <code>io/ioutil</code>) and structured logging with <strong>log/slog</strong>. The chapter ends by turning the statistics application into a cobra tool that stores its data as JSON.</p>`,
    concepts: [
      { term: `stdin, stdout and stderr`, def: `Every UNIX process starts with three open files: standard input, output and error, with <strong>file descriptors 0, 1 and 2</strong> (also reachable as <code>/dev/stdin</code>, <code>/dev/stdout</code>, <code>/dev/stderr</code>). In Go they are <code>os.Stdin</code>, <code>os.Stdout</code> and <code>os.Stderr</code>, all of type <code>*os.File</code>.`, why: `UNIX treats almost everything as a file, and file descriptors are small integers the kernel uses to refer to them. Using the <code>os</code> variables rather than device paths is safer and portable (it also works on Windows). Send diagnostics to stderr so that stdout can be piped.`, ex: `<code>fmt.Fprintln(os.Stderr, "error:", err)</code> writes to fd 2 and leaves the data on stdout clean for <code>| head</code>.` },
      { term: `Programs, processes and threads`, def: `A <strong>program</strong> is a binary on disk. A <strong>process</strong> is a running execution environment built from it: instructions, user data, system data and runtime resources, identified by a <strong>process ID</strong>. Processes are <em>user</em>, <em>daemon</em> (background, no terminal) or <em>kernel</em> processes.`, why: `C creates processes with <code>fork(2)</code>. Go can start new processes through <code>os/exec</code>, but it gives you <strong>no direct control over threads</strong>. You create goroutines, and the Go runtime multiplexes them onto OS threads that it creates and manages itself (Chapter 8).` },
      { term: `io.Reader and io.Writer`, def: `The two one-method interfaces that all file I/O in Go is built on: <code>Read(p []byte) (n int, err error)</code> fills <code>p</code> with up to <code>len(p)</code> bytes, and <code>Write(p []byte) (n int, err error)</code> writes the bytes of <code>p</code>. Both return a byte count and an error.`, why: `Files, network connections, buffers, compressors, hashes and HTTP bodies all satisfy them, so a function that accepts an <code>io.Reader</code> works with every one of them. When a reader runs out of data it returns <code>io.EOF</code>, which marks the expected end of the data rather than a failure.`, ex: `The book's <code>S2</code> type implements <code>Read()</code> over an internal byte slice. Wrapped in <code>bufio.NewReader</code> and read with a 2-byte buffer it prints <code>** 2 He</code>, <code>** 2 ll</code> ... <code>** 1 !</code>. Its <code>S1</code> type shows <em>misuse</em>: a <code>Read()</code> that ignores <code>p</code> and asks the terminal for input instead.`, demo: `c07BufferedIO` },
      { term: `Buffered vs unbuffered I/O (bufio)`, def: `<strong>Unbuffered</strong> I/O hands every read or write straight to the OS. <strong>Buffered</strong> I/O collects data in memory and moves it in large blocks. <code>bufio.Reader</code> and <code>bufio.Writer</code> wrap any <code>io.Reader</code>/<code>io.Writer</code> and add a buffer (4096 bytes by default).`, why: `Buffering reduces the number of <strong>system calls</strong>, which is often the biggest I/O cost. The trade-off: buffered reads can return stale data, and buffered writes are lost if the process dies (or you forget to <code>Flush()</code>) before the buffer is written out. The book suggests unbuffered I/O for critical data.`, demo: `c07BufferedIO` },
      { term: `Reading text: line, word, character`, def: `Create a <code>bufio.NewReader(f)</code> and call <code>r.ReadString('\\n')</code> in a loop: it returns everything up to and <em>including</em> the delimiter. For words, split each line with <code>regexp.MustCompile("[^\\\\s]+").FindAllString(line, -1)</code>. For characters, <code>range</code> over the line, which yields <strong>runes</strong>, not bytes.`, why: `The classic trap: if the last line has no trailing newline, <code>ReadString</code> returns that text <strong>together with</strong> <code>io.EOF</code>. The book's loops check <code>len(line) != 0</code> in the EOF branch so the last line is still processed. <code>bufio.Scanner</code> (an exercise in the book) hides this detail and strips the newline for you.`, ex: `<code>readSize.go</code> reads a fixed amount with <code>f.Read(make([]byte, size))</code> and returns <code>buffer[0:n]</code>, because <code>Read</code> may return fewer bytes than asked for.`, demo: `c07ReadModes` },
      { term: `/dev/random and encoding/binary`, def: `<code>/dev/random</code> is a UNIX device that returns random bytes. The book opens it with <code>os.Open</code> and decodes 8 bytes into an <code>int64</code> seed with <code>binary.Read(f, binary.LittleEndian, &amp;seed)</code>.`, why: `<code>encoding/binary</code> converts raw bytes to fixed-size numbers, and you must choose the <strong>byte order</strong>. For 0x01234567, big endian stores <code>01 23 45 67</code> and little endian stores <code>67 45 23 01</code>. For real secrets, use <code>crypto/rand</code>, which is portable.` },
      { term: `Writing files`, def: `<code>os.Create</code> creates a file or <strong>truncates</strong> an existing one. You can then write with <code>fmt.Fprintf(f, ...)</code>, <code>f.WriteString(s)</code>, a <code>bufio.NewWriter(f)</code> followed by <code>Flush()</code>, or <code>io.WriteString(f, s)</code>. To append, use <code>os.OpenFile(path, os.O_APPEND|os.O_CREATE|os.O_WRONLY, 0644)</code>.`, why: `All four methods write the same 14 bytes in the book's <code>writeFile.go</code>. <code>Write([]byte)</code> is the native form. Strings are easier to work with but add allocations and GC pressure, especially with binary data. Always check the error from <code>Close()</code> or <code>Flush()</code> on files you write.`, demo: `c07BufferedIO` },
      { term: `JSON: Marshal and Unmarshal`, def: `<strong>Marshaling</strong> converts a Go value into JSON bytes (<code>json.Marshal(&amp;v)</code>). <strong>Unmarshaling</strong> parses a JSON byte slice into a Go value through a pointer (<code>json.Unmarshal(data, &amp;v)</code>).`, why: `The book's number one JSON bug: struct fields that are <strong>not exported</strong> (lowercase first letter) are invisible to <code>encoding/json</code>, so they are silently skipped in both directions. When decoding, JSON keys match field names or tag names <strong>case-insensitively</strong>, and unknown keys are ignored.`, ex: `<code>UseAll{Name: "Mike", Surname: "Tsoukalos", Year: 2023}</code> marshals to <code>{"username":"Mike","surname":"Tsoukalos","created":2023}</code>.`, demo: `c07JsonTags` },
      { term: `Struct tags: rename, omitempty, -`, def: `A field tag such as <code>\`json:"username"\`</code> renames the JSON key. <code>\`json:"creationyear,omitempty"\`</code> leaves the field out of the output when it holds its zero value. <code>\`json:"-"\`</code> always excludes it.`, why: `This is how you control the shape of an API payload without renaming Go fields. Use <code>-</code> for secrets such as passwords. Remember that <code>omitempty</code> only checks for the zero value, so an <code>int</code> of 0 or a <code>bool</code> of false disappears too. Use a pointer type if 0 is a meaningful value.`, ex: `<code>NoEmpty{Name: "Mihalis"}</code> marshals to <code>{"username":"Mihalis","surname":""}</code>: the empty <code>Year</code> is omitted, but <code>Surname</code> stays because it has no <code>omitempty</code>.`, demo: `c07JsonTags` },
      { term: `JSON streams and pretty printing`, def: `<code>json.NewEncoder(w).Encode(v)</code> and <code>json.NewDecoder(r).Decode(&amp;v)</code> work directly on an <code>io.Writer</code>/<code>io.Reader</code>, so you don't need to hold the whole document as a byte slice. <code>json.MarshalIndent(v, "", "\\t")</code> and <code>encoder.SetIndent("", "\\t")</code> pretty-print.`, why: `Streams suit files, sockets and HTTP bodies, and a single decoder can read many consecutive JSON values. The book reuses one encoder/decoder (its <code>Serialize</code>/<code>DeSerialize</code> helpers) instead of allocating a new one each time.` },
      { term: `viper: flags, environment, config files`, def: `<code>github.com/spf13/viper</code> unifies configuration. Define flags with <code>pflag.StringP("name", "n", "Mike", "Name parameter")</code>, call <code>pflag.Parse()</code>, then <code>viper.BindPFlags(pflag.CommandLine)</code> and read the values with <code>viper.GetString("name")</code>. <code>viper.BindEnv("GOMAXPROCS")</code> + <code>viper.Get</code> reads environment variables. <code>SetConfigType("json")</code> + <code>SetConfigFile</code> + <code>ReadInConfig()</code> loads JSON, YAML, TOML, HCL or Java properties files.`, why: `It replaces a lot of hand-written parsing. The gotchas: <code>ReadInConfig()</code> behaves like an empty config if the file is missing (its error is easy to ignore), use <code>IsSet()</code> before trusting a key, and <code>viper.Unmarshal(&amp;cfg)</code> uses <strong>mapstructure</strong> tags, not <code>json</code> tags. Normalization functions add flag aliases such as <code>--pass</code> for <code>--password</code>.` },
      { term: `cobra: commands, subcommands, aliases`, def: `<code>github.com/spf13/cobra</code> (the framework behind docker, kubectl and hugo) structures a CLI as a tree of <code>*cobra.Command</code>. <code>cobra-cli init</code> scaffolds <code>main.go</code> and <code>cmd/root.go</code>. <code>cobra-cli add one</code> adds a command, <code>add list -p 'threeCmd'</code> adds a subcommand, and <code>Aliases: []string{"cmd1"}</code> gives a command extra names.`, why: `<strong>Persistent flags</strong> (<code>rootCmd.PersistentFlags()</code>) are inherited by every command. <strong>Local flags</strong> (<code>twoCmd.Flags()</code>) belong to one command, read with <code>cmd.Flags().GetString("username")</code>. Bind them to viper with <code>viper.BindPFlag</code>, or to a variable with <code>BoolVarP(&amp;v, ...)</code>. If two commands share an alias, the compiler won't complain and only the first one runs.` },
      { term: `Embedding files (embed)`, def: `A <code>//go:embed path</code> directive placed right above a package-level variable bakes a file into the binary at build time. The variable can be a <code>string</code>, a <code>[]byte</code> or an <code>embed.FS</code> (for many files or directories). Import with <code>_ "embed"</code> if you don't use the package by name.`, why: `You ship one self-contained binary with its templates, static assets or default config inside, which is ideal for containers. Use <code>[]byte</code> for binary files and <code>string</code> for text. The book's <code>printSource.go</code> even embeds its own source code.` },
      { term: `os.ReadDir, DirEntry and the end of io/ioutil`, def: `<code>io/ioutil</code> has been deprecated since Go 1.16. Its replacements are <code>os.ReadFile</code>, <code>os.WriteFile</code>, <code>os.MkdirTemp</code>, <code>os.CreateTemp</code> and <code>os.ReadDir</code>. <code>os.ReadDir</code> returns <code>[]os.DirEntry</code> instead of <code>[]FileInfo</code>.`, why: `A <code>DirEntry</code> carries the name and type without an extra <code>stat</code> call for every entry. Call <code>entry.Info()</code> only when you need the size or mode. The same idea makes <code>filepath.WalkDir</code> faster than the old <code>filepath.Walk</code>.`, ex: `The book's <code>GetSize()</code> recurses with <code>os.ReadDir</code>, sums <code>info.Size()</code> for files, and reports the total size of <code>/usr/bin</code>.` },
      { term: `io/fs: a read-only file system interface`, def: `<code>io/fs</code> defines <code>fs.FS</code>, an abstract read-only file system, plus helpers such as <code>fs.WalkDir</code>, <code>fs.ReadFile</code> and <code>fs.Stat</code>. <code>embed.FS</code>, <code>os.DirFS(dir)</code> and <code>testing/fstest.MapFS</code> all implement it.`, why: `Code written against <code>fs.FS</code> works the same on embedded files, real directories, zip archives or in-memory test fixtures. The book's <code>ioFS.go</code> lists, searches and extracts files from an embedded <code>./static</code> directory.` },
      { term: `log/slog and io.Discard`, def: `<code>log/slog</code> (Go 1.21) is the standard library's <strong>structured logger</strong>. It has levels (Debug, Info, Warn, Error), a <code>TextHandler</code> that writes key=value output and a <code>JSONHandler</code> that writes JSON. A <code>slog.LevelVar</code> changes the level at runtime. <code>io.Discard</code> is an <code>io.Writer</code> that throws everything away.`, why: `JSON logs can be shipped to a log store and queried. The default level is INFO, so <code>Debug</code> calls print nothing until you lower it. <code>log.SetOutput(io.Discard)</code> or <code>slog.NewJSONHandler(io.Discard, nil)</code> turns logging off without touching the call sites, which is how the stats app implements its <code>--log</code> flag.` }
    ],
    cheatsheet: [
      { title: `Opening and writing files`, type: `table`, headers: [`Call`, `What it does`, `Notes`], rows: [
        [`<code>os.Open(p)</code>`, `Open read-only`, `Returns <code>*os.File</code> (an io.Reader); <code>defer f.Close()</code>`],
        [`<code>os.Create(p)</code>`, `Create or <strong>truncate</strong>, read-write`, `Mode 0666 before umask`],
        [`<code>os.OpenFile(p, os.O_APPEND|os.O_CREATE|os.O_WRONLY, 0644)</code>`, `Append, creating if needed`, `Flags are OR-ed; the perm applies only on create`],
        [`<code>fmt.Fprintf(w, ...)</code>`, `Formatted write to any io.Writer`, `Works on files, buffers, sockets, os.Stdout`],
        [`<code>f.WriteString(s)</code> / <code>io.WriteString(w, s)</code>`, `Write a string`, `Easy to use, but converting strings to bytes adds allocations`],
        [`<code>bufio.NewWriter(f)</code>`, `Buffered writer (4 KB default)`, `Must call <code>Flush()</code> or the tail of the data is lost`],
        [`<code>os.WriteFile(p, b, 0644)</code> / <code>os.ReadFile(p)</code>`, `Whole-file write / read`, `Replacements for <code>ioutil.WriteFile</code>/<code>ReadFile</code>`]
      ] },
      { title: `io/ioutil migration (Go 1.16+)`, type: `table`, headers: [`Deprecated`, `Use instead`], rows: [
        [`<code>ioutil.ReadFile</code>`, `<code>os.ReadFile</code>`],
        [`<code>ioutil.WriteFile</code>`, `<code>os.WriteFile</code>`],
        [`<code>ioutil.ReadDir</code> (returns []FileInfo)`, `<code>os.ReadDir</code> (returns []DirEntry; call <code>Info()</code> when needed)`],
        [`<code>ioutil.TempDir</code>`, `<code>os.MkdirTemp</code>`],
        [`<code>ioutil.TempFile</code>`, `<code>os.CreateTemp</code>`],
        [`<code>ioutil.ReadAll</code> / <code>ioutil.Discard</code>`, `<code>io.ReadAll</code> / <code>io.Discard</code>`],
        [`<code>filepath.Walk</code> (FileInfo)`, `<code>filepath.WalkDir</code> / <code>fs.WalkDir</code> (DirEntry, faster)`]
      ] },
      { title: `encoding/json struct tags`, type: `table`, headers: [`Field declaration`, `Marshal behaviour`], rows: [
        [`<code>Name string</code>`, `Key <code>"Name"</code>; Unmarshal also accepts <code>"name"</code>, <code>"NAME"</code> (case-insensitive)`],
        [`<code>Name string \`json:"username"\`</code>`, `Key renamed to <code>"username"</code>`],
        [`<code>Year int \`json:"year,omitempty"\`</code>`, `Omitted when the value is the zero value (0, "", nil, false, empty slice/map)`],
        [`<code>Pass string \`json:"-"\`</code>`, `Never encoded or decoded`],
        [`<code>secret string</code>`, `Unexported: invisible to encoding/json in both directions`],
        [`<code>ID int \`json:"id,string"\`</code>`, `Encodes the number as a JSON string, <code>"42"</code>`]
      ], note: `viper.Unmarshal ignores json tags. It uses mapstructure tags such as \`mapstructure:"macos"\`.` },
      { title: `Idiomatic file and JSON code`, type: `code`, lang: `go`, body: `// Read a file line by line (newline stripped, last line handled)
f, err := os.Open(path)
if err != nil {
    return err
}
defer f.Close()
sc := bufio.NewScanner(f)
for sc.Scan() {
    fmt.Println(sc.Text())
}
if err := sc.Err(); err != nil {
    return err
}

// Buffered write: always Flush
w := bufio.NewWriter(out)
defer w.Flush()
fmt.Fprintf(w, "%d records\\n", n)

// JSON with tags
type User struct {
    Name string \`json:"username"\`
    Year int    \`json:"year,omitempty"\`
    Pass string \`json:"-"\`
}
b, err := json.Marshal(User{Name: "Mike"}) // {"username":"Mike"}

// Stream many records
enc := json.NewEncoder(os.Stdout)
enc.SetIndent("", "  ")
_ = enc.Encode(records)

// Embed a file into the binary
//go:embed static/config.json
var defaultConfig []byte` },
      { title: `CLI and logging quick facts`, type: `list`, items: [
        `<strong>pflag</strong> gives POSIX-style <code>--name</code>/<code>-n</code> flags. <code>pflag.Parse()</code> runs after all flags are defined, then <code>viper.BindPFlags(pflag.CommandLine)</code>.`,
        `<strong>viper</strong> lookup order: explicit <code>Set</code>, then flag, env, config file, key/value store, default.`,
        `<strong>cobra</strong>: <code>Use</code>, <code>Short</code>, <code>Long</code>, <code>Aliases</code>, <code>Run</code>. <code>PersistentFlags()</code> are inherited by all commands; <code>Flags()</code> are local to one.`,
        `<code>go run main.go two list</code> passes <code>list</code> as an <em>argument</em> to <code>two</code>. It is only a subcommand if it was added under that parent.`,
        `<strong>slog</strong>: <code>slog.New(slog.NewJSONHandler(os.Stderr, &amp;slog.HandlerOptions{Level: lv}))</code>; <code>slog.SetDefault(logger)</code> also redirects the top-level <code>slog.Info</code> calls.`,
        `<code>SIGKILL</code>/<code>SIGSTOP</code> can never be caught (Chapter 8). Write logs to <strong>stderr</strong> and data to stdout.`
      ] }
    ],
    interview: [
      { q: `What are io.Reader and io.Writer, and why are they so central in Go?`, a: `<p>They are one-method interfaces: <code>Read(p []byte) (n int, err error)</code> and <code>Write(p []byte) (n int, err error)</code>. Files, sockets, <code>bytes.Buffer</code>, <code>strings.Reader</code>, gzip streams, hashes, HTTP request and response bodies and <code>os.Stdout</code> all implement them.</p><p>Because Go interfaces are satisfied implicitly, any function written against <code>io.Reader</code> works with all of these, and you can stack them: <code>bufio.NewReader(gzip.NewReader(resp.Body))</code>. Helpers such as <code>io.Copy</code>, <code>io.ReadAll</code>, <code>io.MultiWriter</code>, <code>io.TeeReader</code> and <code>io.LimitReader</code> build on the same two methods.</p>`, diff: `easy` },
      { q: `What are the rules a correct Read implementation must follow?`, a: `<ul><li>It may return <strong>fewer bytes than len(p)</strong> (<code>n &lt; len(p)</code>) without an error, so callers must use <code>p[:n]</code>.</li><li>At the end of the data it returns <code>0, io.EOF</code>. It may also return <code>n &gt; 0</code> <em>together with</em> <code>io.EOF</code>, so callers should process the n bytes before looking at the error.</li><li>It should not hold on to <code>p</code> after returning.</li><li><code>io.EOF</code> is a sentinel value, not a failure. Compare it with <code>err == io.EOF</code> or <code>errors.Is(err, io.EOF)</code>.</li></ul><p>The book's <code>S2.Read</code> copies bytes until the buffer is full or the data runs out, then returns <code>io.EOF</code> on the next call. Its <code>S1.Read</code> deliberately breaks the contract by reading from the terminal instead of filling <code>p</code>.</p>`, diff: `med` },
      { q: `Buffered vs unbuffered I/O: what is the trade-off, and what is the classic bufio.Writer bug?`, a: `<p>Without a buffer, every <code>Write</code> becomes a system call. With 1-byte writes, a 1 MB file costs about a million syscalls. A <code>bufio.Writer</code> with a 4 KB buffer issues about 256. Buffering adds latency and a window in which data lives only in process memory: buffered writes can be lost on a crash, and buffered reads can be stale.</p><p>The classic bug is forgetting <code>w.Flush()</code>. The last partial buffer never reaches the file, so the output is silently truncated. Use <code>defer w.Flush()</code> and check its error, and remember that <code>f.Close()</code> does <em>not</em> flush a bufio.Writer that wraps the file.</p>`, diff: `med` },
      { q: `You read a file with bufio.Reader.ReadString('\\n') and the last line goes missing. Why?`, a: `<p>If the file doesn't end with a newline, <code>ReadString</code> returns the final text <strong>and</strong> <code>io.EOF</code> in the same call. A loop that does <code>if err == io.EOF { break }</code> before using <code>line</code> throws that last line away.</p><p>The book handles it inside the EOF branch with <code>if len(line) != 0 { process(line) }</code>. The simpler fix is <code>bufio.Scanner</code>, which returns the last token whether or not it ends in a newline and strips the delimiter. Watch out for Scanner's default 64 KB token limit (raise it with <code>sc.Buffer</code>), and remember that <code>ReadString</code> keeps the <code>\\n</code> in the result.</p>`, diff: `med` },
      { q: `Why might json.Marshal produce {} for a struct that clearly has data?`, a: `<p>Because the fields are <strong>unexported</strong> (they start with a lowercase letter). <code>encoding/json</code> uses reflection and can only see exported fields, so <code>type user struct{ name string }</code> marshals to <code>{}</code> and unmarshals into nothing, with no error. The book calls this the number one JSON bug. The fix is to export the field and use a tag for the key: <code>Name string \`json:"name"\`</code>.</p><p>Other common surprises: <code>omitempty</code> drops legitimate zero values (0, false), decoding matches keys case-insensitively, unknown keys are ignored unless you call <code>Decoder.DisallowUnknownFields()</code>, and numbers decoded into <code>interface{}</code> become <code>float64</code>.</p>`, diff: `easy` },
      { q: `Explain omitempty and "-" with an example. How do you keep a zero value that is meaningful?`, a: `<p><code>\`json:"year,omitempty"\`</code> skips the field when it holds the zero value for its type. <code>\`json:"-"\`</code> always skips it (the book uses it for a password). The book's <code>Password{Name: "Mihalis", Pass: "myPassword"}</code> marshals to just <code>{"username":"Mihalis"}</code>: Pass is excluded, and the empty Surname and Year are omitted.</p><p>If 0 or false carries meaning (a discount of 0, <code>active: false</code>), use a pointer field such as <code>*int</code> with omitempty: <code>nil</code> means "absent" and a pointer to 0 is encoded as 0. Note that omitempty has no effect on struct values. Go 1.24 added <code>omitzero</code> for that case.</p>`, diff: `med` },
      { q: `When should you use json.Encoder/Decoder instead of Marshal/Unmarshal?`, a: `<p>Use <code>Marshal</code>/<code>Unmarshal</code> when the whole document is already in memory as <code>[]byte</code>. Use <code>json.NewDecoder(r)</code> and <code>json.NewEncoder(w)</code> when you work with an <code>io.Reader</code>/<code>io.Writer</code> such as an HTTP body, a file or a socket: you avoid an intermediate buffer, you can decode a <strong>sequence</strong> of JSON values (or NDJSON) by calling <code>Decode</code> in a loop, and you can stream large arrays with <code>Token()</code>.</p><p>The encoder also supports <code>SetIndent</code> (pretty printing) and <code>SetEscapeHTML(false)</code>. <code>Encode</code> appends a newline after each value.</p>`, diff: `med` },
      { q: `What problems do viper and cobra solve, and how do they fit together?`, a: `<p><strong>cobra</strong> provides structure: a tree of commands and subcommands (<code>app three list</code>), aliases, generated help and usage, shell completion, and two flag scopes: <em>persistent</em> flags inherited by all subcommands and <em>local</em> flags for one command.</p><p><strong>viper</strong> provides configuration: one place to read values that may come from flags (via pflag), environment variables, config files (JSON, YAML, TOML, HCL, properties) or defaults, with a defined precedence. It can also <code>Unmarshal</code> into a struct using <code>mapstructure</code> tags.</p><p>You bind cobra's flags to viper (<code>viper.BindPFlag("directory", rootCmd.PersistentFlags().Lookup("directory"))</code>), so a value can come from <code>--directory</code>, <code>$APP_DIRECTORY</code> or the config file without the command needing to know which.</p>`, diff: `med` },
      { q: `What does //go:embed do and what are its rules?`, a: `<p>It tells the compiler to copy files into the binary at build time and expose them through a package-level variable of type <code>string</code>, <code>[]byte</code> or <code>embed.FS</code>. The directive must sit directly above the variable, the package must import <code>embed</code> (as <code>_ "embed"</code> if unused), and paths are relative to the source file's directory and cannot use <code>..</code> to leave the module. Patterns and directories work only with <code>embed.FS</code>, and files whose names start with <code>.</code> or <code>_</code> are skipped unless you use the <code>all:</code> prefix.</p><p><code>embed.FS</code> implements <code>fs.FS</code>, so it works with <code>fs.WalkDir</code>, <code>fs.ReadFile</code>, <code>http.FS</code> and <code>template.ParseFS</code>. The result is a single, self-contained binary.</p>`, diff: `med` },
      { q: `Why was io/ioutil deprecated, and why is os.ReadDir faster than ioutil.ReadDir?`, a: `<p><code>io/ioutil</code> was a grab bag of helpers that belonged elsewhere, so Go 1.16 moved them to <code>os</code> and <code>io</code> (<code>os.ReadFile</code>, <code>os.WriteFile</code>, <code>os.MkdirTemp</code>, <code>os.CreateTemp</code>, <code>io.ReadAll</code>, <code>io.Discard</code>). Old code still compiles.</p><p><code>ioutil.ReadDir</code> returned <code>[]FileInfo</code>, which meant an <code>lstat</code> per entry, and sorted the result. <code>os.ReadDir</code> returns <code>[]DirEntry</code>, built from what the directory read itself reports (name and type). The expensive <code>Info()</code> call happens only for the entries you ask about, which matters for large trees. That is also why <code>filepath.WalkDir</code> beats <code>filepath.Walk</code>.</p>`, diff: `hard` },
      { q: `How does log/slog differ from the log package, and how would you disable logging cheaply?`, a: `<p><code>log</code> prints unstructured text lines. <code>slog</code> adds <strong>levels</strong>, <strong>key-value attributes</strong> (<code>slog.Info("saved", "records", n)</code>) and pluggable <strong>handlers</strong>: <code>TextHandler</code> (key=value) and <code>JSONHandler</code> (one JSON object per line, ready for a log pipeline). A <code>LevelVar</code> changes the level at runtime, and <code>logger.With(...)</code> adds context such as a request ID.</p><p>To disable output, point the handler at <code>io.Discard</code> (the book's <code>--log</code> flag does this) or raise the level so that <code>Enabled()</code> rejects records early. Raising the level is cheaper, because it skips formatting entirely.</p>`, diff: `easy` }
    ],
    labs: [`c07BufferedIO`, `c07ReadModes`, `c07JsonTags`]
  },

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------
  {
    id: `ch8`, part: 3, number: 8,
    title: `Go Concurrency`,
    overview: `<p>The most interview-relevant chapter of the book. A <strong>goroutine</strong> is the smallest unit of execution in Go. Goroutines live inside OS threads, which live inside a process, and the runtime's <strong>m:n scheduler</strong> multiplexes many goroutines (G) onto a few OS threads (M) through <strong>GOMAXPROCS</strong> logical processors (P). Each P has a local run queue, a global queue backs them up, and idle Ps <strong>steal work</strong> from busy ones. Concurrency is a way to structure a program; parallelism is what you may get when the structure allows it.</p><p>The chapter then covers the tools one by one: <code>go</code> and <strong>sync.WaitGroup</strong> (and what happens when Add and Done don't match), <strong>channels</strong> (unbuffered, buffered, closed, nil, directional), the <strong>race detector</strong>, <strong>select</strong> and timeouts with <code>time.After</code>, worker pools, signal channels that fix the order of execution, <strong>UNIX signal handling</strong>, shared memory with <strong>Mutex</strong>, <strong>RWMutex</strong> and <strong>atomic</strong>, the <strong>monitor goroutine</strong> pattern ("share memory by communicating"), the loop-variable closure bug fixed in Go 1.22, the <strong>context</strong> package (WithCancel, WithTimeout, WithDeadline and the newer WithCancelCause) and the weighted <strong>semaphore</strong>. The statistics application becomes about 3x faster with one goroutine per file and no channels at all.</p>`,
    concepts: [
      { term: `Processes, threads and goroutines`, def: `A <strong>process</strong> is a running program with its own memory, file descriptors and other resources. A <strong>thread</strong> is a lighter flow of control with its own stack, inside a process. A <strong>goroutine</strong> is the smallest unit Go can run concurrently. It is not autonomous: it lives in an OS thread, inside a process.`, why: `Goroutines start with a tiny stack that grows on demand (a few KB vs about 1-8 MB for a thread), start fast and switch in user space, so hundreds of thousands are fine. The OS schedules processes and threads; the Go runtime schedules goroutines.`, ex: `Every Go program has at least one goroutine, the one running <code>main()</code>. When <code>main</code> returns, the program exits, even if other goroutines are still running.` },
      { term: `The Go scheduler: G, M and P`, def: `The runtime uses <strong>m:n scheduling</strong>: m goroutines run on n OS threads. The three entities are <strong>G</strong> (goroutine), <strong>M</strong> (machine, an OS thread) and <strong>P</strong> (logical processor, holding a local run queue). An M must hold a P to run Go code, so at most <code>GOMAXPROCS</code> goroutines execute at the same moment.`, why: `A goroutine is <em>executing</em> (on an M), <em>runnable</em> (in a queue) or <em>waiting</em> (blocked on a channel, mutex, I/O or syscall). When a G blocks in a syscall, its M blocks with it and the P is handed to another M, so the runtime can create more threads than GOMAXPROCS. Network I/O, in contrast, parks the G on the netpoller without blocking a thread.`, demo: `c08Scheduler` },
      { term: `Run queues and work stealing`, def: `Each P has a <strong>local run queue</strong>; there is also a <strong>global run queue</strong>. A new goroutine goes onto the creating P's local queue. A P with no work checks the global queue (which is also checked periodically for fairness) and, if that is empty too, <strong>steals</strong> half of another P's local queue.`, why: `Fair, even distribution of load sounds ideal, but tasks depend on each other, so some processors end up idle. Work stealing keeps every P busy without a central lock. The book adds that Go uses the <strong>fork-join</strong> model and steals <em>continuations</em> rather than tasks. A <code>go</code> statement forks; <code>wg.Wait()</code> or receiving the results from a channel is a join.`, demo: `c08Scheduler` },
      { term: `GOMAXPROCS`, def: `The number of OS threads that can execute user-level Go code <strong>simultaneously</strong>, which equals the number of Ps. Since Go 1.5 it defaults to the number of logical CPUs. <code>runtime.GOMAXPROCS(0)</code> reads the value without changing it; <code>runtime.GOMAXPROCS(n)</code> with n ≥ 1 sets it and returns the old value.`, why: `It limits how many threads <em>run</em> Go code, not how many threads exist (threads blocked in syscalls don't count). Setting it lower than the number of cores wastes them, and setting it higher mostly adds context switches. In containers with CPU quotas, older Go versions over-provisioned (hence <code>uber-go/automaxprocs</code>); Go 1.25 made the default respect cgroup CPU limits.`, ex: `<code>GOMAXPROCS=100 go run maxprocs.go</code> prints <code>GOMAXPROCS: 100</code>.`, demo: `c08Scheduler` },
      { term: `Concurrency vs parallelism`, def: `<strong>Parallelism</strong> is executing several things at the same instant. <strong>Concurrency</strong> is structuring a program as independently executing components, which <em>may</em> run in parallel if the hardware and OS allow it.`, why: `A good concurrent design gives you parallelism for free when you add cores, and it improves the design and data flow even on one core. Think about independent components, not about cores. Concurrency also has costs: complexity, harder benchmarking and the risk of exhausting shared resources, which is why the book adds it last to the statistics app.` },
      { term: `Creating goroutines and waiting with sync.WaitGroup`, def: `<code>go f(x)</code> or <code>go func(x int){...}(10)</code> starts a goroutine and returns immediately. Nothing about execution order is guaranteed. To wait, call <code>wg.Add(1)</code> <strong>before</strong> the <code>go</code> statement, <code>defer wg.Done()</code> inside the goroutine and <code>wg.Wait()</code> at the join point.`, why: `<code>time.Sleep</code> in main is a hack and races with the goroutines. If you call <code>Add</code> inside the goroutine, <code>Wait</code> may return before the goroutine has registered. If Add and Done don't match, more Dones gives <code>panic: sync: negative WaitGroup counter</code>, and fewer gives <code>fatal error: all goroutines are asleep - deadlock!</code>. A WaitGroup must not be copied after first use, so pass <code>*sync.WaitGroup</code>.`, ex: `Go 1.25 adds <code>wg.Go(func(){...})</code>, which does the Add/Done bookkeeping for you.`, demo: `c08Race` },
      { term: `Channels: send, receive, close, range`, def: `<code>ch := make(chan int)</code> creates an unbuffered channel with element type int; <code>make(chan int, 5)</code> creates a buffered one. <code>ch &lt;- v</code> sends, <code>v := &lt;-ch</code> receives and <code>v, ok := &lt;-ch</code> also reports whether the channel is still open. <code>close(ch)</code> marks the end of the data, and <code>for v := range ch</code> loops until the channel is closed and drained.`, why: `An <strong>unbuffered</strong> send blocks until a receiver takes the value. This rendezvous is a synchronization point as well as a data transfer. Only the <strong>sender</strong> should close a channel, and only when no more sends can happen (the book's <code>channels.go</code> closes on the receiving side and panics now and then). You don't need to close a channel for it to be garbage collected; close it to signal "no more data".`, ex: `Direction types in signatures: <code>func printer(ch chan&lt;- bool)</code> can only send, <code>func f2(out &lt;-chan int, in chan&lt;- int)</code> receives from <code>out</code> and sends to <code>in</code>. Misuse is a compile error.`, demo: `c08Channels` },
      { term: `Closed and nil channels`, def: `On a <strong>closed</strong> channel, a receive returns any buffered values, then the zero value with <code>ok == false</code>, immediately and forever. A send panics (<code>send on closed channel</code>), and so does a second close. On a <strong>nil</strong> channel (the zero value of a channel type), send and receive <strong>block forever</strong>, and close panics (<code>close of nil channel</code>).`, why: `Receiving from a closed channel never blocks, which is what makes <code>close(done)</code> a broadcast to every waiting goroutine. "Nil blocks forever" sounds like a bug, but it is useful inside <code>select</code>: setting a channel variable to nil disables that case, as in the book's <code>nilChannel.go</code>, where <code>add()</code> sets <code>c = nil</code> when its timer fires.`, demo: `c08Channels` },
      { term: `Data races and the race detector`, def: `A <strong>data race</strong> happens when two goroutines access the same memory location concurrently, at least one access is a write, and nothing orders the accesses. <code>go run -race</code>, <code>go build -race</code> and <code>go test -race</code> build an instrumented binary that records memory accesses and synchronization events and prints <code>WARNING: DATA RACE</code> with both stacks.`, why: `Races give different results on each run and often appear only under load, which makes them very hard to debug. The detector finds only races that actually happen in the run, so run your tests with <code>-race</code> in CI. A program that reports races exits with status 66. A plain <code>counter++</code> is three steps (load, add, store), so concurrent increments lose updates.`, demo: `c08Race` },
      { term: `select`, def: `<code>select</code> waits on several channel operations at once. If none is ready it blocks; if several are ready it picks one <strong>uniformly at random</strong>; a <code>default</code> case makes it non-blocking. <code>select{}</code> blocks forever.`, why: `It is the building block for timeouts, cancellation, non-blocking sends and receives, fan-in and priority handling. Because the choice is random, cases are not checked in order. To give one case priority you need a nested select or an extra check. Always plan an exit path: the book's <code>gen()</code> forgets to <code>return</code> on <code>&lt;-end</code> and is rescued only by its <code>time.After</code> case.`, demo: `c08Channels` },
      { term: `Timeouts with time.After`, def: `<code>select { case r := &lt;-c1: ...; case &lt;-time.After(time.Second): fmt.Println("timeout c1") }</code> gives up waiting after a duration. The book shows it inline in <code>main()</code> (<code>timeOut1.go</code>) and in a helper that takes the duration as a parameter (<code>timeOut2.go</code>).`, why: `A timeout stops <em>waiting</em>. It doesn't stop the work: the slow goroutine keeps running, and if it sends on an unbuffered channel that nobody reads anymore, it leaks. Give it a buffered channel of size 1 or a context. In a loop, <code>time.After</code> creates a new timer on every iteration, so prefer a reusable <code>time.Timer</code> or <code>context.WithTimeout</code>.`, demo: `c08Context` },
      { term: `Buffered channels`, def: `<code>make(chan T, n)</code> has room for n values: sends block only when the buffer is full, and receives block only when it is empty. <code>len(ch)</code> and <code>cap(ch)</code> report the current number of values and the capacity.`, why: `Buffered channels decouple producers from consumers and absorb bursts. A buffered channel of size N also works as a <strong>counting semaphore</strong> that limits concurrency. Combined with <code>select</code>/<code>default</code>, it can drop work when full: the book's <code>bufChannel.go</code> prints <code>No space for 5 ... 9</code>. A bigger buffer doesn't fix a slow consumer; it only delays the backpressure.`, demo: `c08Channels` },
      { term: `Worker pools`, def: `A fixed set of goroutines reads jobs from a shared channel. In the book's <code>wPools.go</code>, <code>create()</code> sends <code>Client</code> jobs on <code>clients</code> and closes it, each <code>worker()</code> ranges over <code>clients</code> and sends a <code>Result</code> on <code>data</code>, <code>wg.Wait(); close(data)</code> runs after all workers return, and a collector goroutine signals <code>finished</code>.`, why: `It bounds the number of concurrent operations (database connections, CPU-heavy work) and protects the server from overload. Ownership rules prevent panics: the producer closes the jobs channel, and the coordinator closes the results channel after <code>Wait()</code>, never a worker. Total time is roughly ⌈jobs ÷ workers⌉ × job time once the workers become the bottleneck.`, demo: `c08WorkerPool` },
      { term: `Signal channels and ordering goroutines`, def: `A <strong>signal channel</strong> carries an event rather than data, usually a <code>chan struct{}</code> (zero bytes per value) that you <code>close()</code>. In <code>defineOrder.go</code> each function blocks on <code>&lt;-a</code> and closes <code>b</code> when it finishes, which forces the order A → B → C → D, whatever order the goroutines were started in.`, why: `Closing a channel wakes <em>every</em> receiver, so it is a one-to-many broadcast, while a send wakes exactly one. That is why <code>D()</code> can run four times but A, B and C only once each: a channel can be closed only once. The same idea is behind <code>ctx.Done()</code>. The book notes this technique is for a small number of goroutines.` },
      { term: `Handling UNIX signals`, def: `<code>sigs := make(chan os.Signal, 1)</code>; <code>signal.Notify(sigs, syscall.SIGINT, syscall.SIGTERM)</code> (with no signal arguments, Notify relays every signal); then a dedicated goroutine loops on <code>sig := &lt;-sigs</code> and uses a <code>switch</code> to handle each signal.`, why: `The channel must be buffered: <code>signal.Notify</code> does not block when it sends, so an unbuffered channel can miss a signal. <code>SIGKILL</code> and <code>SIGSTOP</code> can never be caught, blocked or ignored (<code>kill -9</code> always wins). <code>SIGINFO</code> doesn't exist on Linux; use <code>SIGUSR1</code>/<code>SIGUSR2</code>. Today <code>signal.NotifyContext</code> gives you a context that is canceled on SIGINT/SIGTERM, ideal for graceful shutdown.` },
      { term: `sync.Mutex and critical sections`, def: `A <strong>critical section</strong> is code that must not run in more than one goroutine at a time. <code>m.Lock()</code> / <code>defer m.Unlock()</code> around it gives mutual exclusion; the book compares a mutex to a buffered channel of capacity 1. The zero value is an unlocked mutex.`, why: `If you forget to unlock, every other <code>Lock()</code> blocks forever, which ends in <code>fatal error: all goroutines are asleep - deadlock!</code> if nothing else can run. Go mutexes are <strong>not reentrant</strong>, so locking the same mutex twice in one goroutine deadlocks; the book warns against nesting critical sections and spreading locks across functions. Don't copy a struct that contains a mutex (<code>go vet</code> catches it). Reads need the lock too.`, demo: `c08Race` },
      { term: `sync.RWMutex`, def: `A reader/writer lock: any number of goroutines can hold <code>RLock()</code> at the same time, but <code>Lock()</code> is exclusive and waits until all readers have called <code>RUnlock()</code>.`, why: `It helps read-heavy shared state such as caches and configuration. Once a writer is waiting, new readers block, so writers aren't starved. In the book's <code>rwMutex.go</code>, <code>Change()</code> waits while the <code>show()</code> readers hold the lock. Never modify data under <code>RLock</code>. For short critical sections with few readers, a plain Mutex is often faster because RWMutex costs more bookkeeping.` },
      { term: `sync/atomic`, def: `Atomic operations complete in one indivisible step: <code>atomic.AddInt64(&amp;c.val, 1)</code>, <code>LoadInt64</code>, <code>StoreInt64</code>, <code>CompareAndSwapInt64</code>. Since Go 1.19 there are also typed values: <code>atomic.Int64</code>, <code>atomic.Bool</code>, <code>atomic.Pointer[T]</code>.`, why: `For a single counter or flag they are cheaper than a mutex and race-free: the book's <code>atomic.go</code> runs 100 goroutines × 4 increments and reliably prints 400 under <code>-race</code>. The rule: once a variable is accessed atomically, every read and write must be atomic. Atomics don't compose, though: if two fields must change together, you need a mutex.`, demo: `c08Race` },
      { term: `Monitor goroutine (share by communicating)`, def: `A single goroutine owns the state, and everyone else talks to it over channels. In the book's <code>monitor.go</code>, <code>monitor()</code> loops on <code>select { case v := &lt;-writeValue: value = v; case readValue &lt;- value: }</code>, and <code>set()</code> and <code>read()</code> just send to or receive from those channels.`, why: `This is Go's proverb "Do not communicate by sharing memory; share memory by communicating." Only one goroutine touches <code>value</code>, so no lock is needed and races are impossible by construction. It costs a goroutine and a channel hop per operation, and the author prefers it for clarity.` },
      { term: `Closured variables and the go statement`, def: `A goroutine's closure captures <em>variables</em>, not their values. Before Go 1.22, <code>for i := 0; i &lt;= 20; i++ { go func(){ fmt.Print(i) }() }</code> shared one <code>i</code>, so most goroutines printed 21, and <code>-race</code> reported a data race.`, why: `The classic fixes are to pass the value as an argument (<code>go func(x int){...}(i)</code>) or to shadow it with <code>i := i</code>. Since <strong>Go 1.22</strong> each loop iteration gets a fresh variable (if the module's go.mod says go 1.22 or later), so the bug is gone for loop variables. The same capture rule still applies to any other variable that a closure shares with its creator.` },
      { term: `The context package`, def: `<code>context.Context</code> is an interface with <code>Deadline()</code>, <code>Done()</code>, <code>Err()</code> and <code>Value()</code>. Starting from <code>context.Background()</code> or <code>context.TODO()</code>, you derive children with <code>WithCancel</code>, <code>WithTimeout</code> or <code>WithDeadline</code>, each of which returns the child plus a <code>CancelFunc</code>.`, why: `Canceling a context closes its <code>Done()</code> channel and cancels <strong>all its descendants</strong>, never its parent. <code>Err()</code> then returns <code>context.Canceled</code> or <code>context.DeadlineExceeded</code>. Always <code>defer cancel()</code>: it releases the child's timer and the parent's reference to it, otherwise you leak them until the parent ends. Pass ctx as the first parameter and don't store it in structs.`, ex: `The book's <code>f1</code>/<code>f2</code>/<code>f3</code> each race <code>&lt;-ctx.Done()</code> against <code>time.After(t)</code>. With a delay of 3 they all print a time; with 13 all three print <code>Done: context canceled</code>.`, demo: `c08Context` },
      { term: `WithCancelCause and context.Cause`, def: `<code>ctx, cancel := context.WithCancelCause(parent)</code> returns a <code>CancelCauseFunc</code> that takes an error: <code>cancel(errors.New("Canceled by timeout"))</code>. <code>context.Cause(ctx)</code> returns that error, while <code>ctx.Err()</code> still returns <code>context.Canceled</code>. Go 1.21 added <code>WithTimeoutCause</code> and <code>WithDeadlineCause</code>.`, why: `Plain cancellation only says "canceled". With a cause, the code that observes the cancellation can learn <em>why</em> (client went away, quota exceeded, shutdown) and log or return it. Children inherit the parent's cause.`, demo: `c08Context` },
      { term: `Weighted semaphore`, def: `<code>golang.org/x/sync/semaphore</code>: <code>sem := semaphore.NewWeighted(4)</code>, then <code>sem.Acquire(ctx, 1)</code> before starting each goroutine and <code>defer sem.Release(1)</code> inside it. Acquire blocks until enough weight is free or the context is done.`, why: `It limits concurrency without a jobs channel. Each goroutine writes to its own <code>results[i]</code>, so there is no race. The book's trick at the end, <code>sem.Acquire(ctx, int64(Workers))</code>, grabs all the weight and therefore waits for every worker, like <code>Wait()</code>. Weights let a heavy job take several slots. <code>errgroup.SetLimit</code> is a common alternative.`, demo: `c08WorkerPool` }
    ],
    cheatsheet: [
      { title: `Channel operation outcomes`, type: `table`, headers: [`Operation`, `nil channel`, `open channel`, `closed channel`], rows: [
        [`<code>ch &lt;- v</code>`, `blocks forever`, `blocks until a receiver takes it (unbuffered) or while the buffer is full`, `<strong>panic</strong>: send on closed channel`],
        [`<code>v := &lt;-ch</code>`, `blocks forever`, `blocks until a value is available`, `drains buffered values, then returns the zero value immediately`],
        [`<code>v, ok := &lt;-ch</code>`, `blocks forever`, `<code>ok == true</code>`, `<code>ok == false</code> once the buffer is drained`],
        [`<code>close(ch)</code>`, `<strong>panic</strong>: close of nil channel`, `wakes all receivers; blocked senders panic`, `<strong>panic</strong>: close of closed channel`],
        [`<code>len(ch)</code> / <code>cap(ch)</code>`, `0 / 0`, `values queued / buffer size`, `values left / buffer size`],
        [`<code>for v := range ch</code>`, `blocks forever`, `loops until the channel is closed`, `drains what is left, then exits`]
      ], note: `Only the sender closes. A receive-only channel (&lt;-chan T) cannot be closed: it is a compile error.` },
      { title: `Choosing a synchronization tool`, type: `table`, headers: [`Tool`, `Use it when`, `Watch out for`], rows: [
        [`<code>sync.WaitGroup</code>`, `Waiting for N goroutines to finish (join)`, `Add before go; Done count must match; pass by pointer`],
        [`unbuffered channel`, `Handing off data with synchronization`, `Both sides block; goroutine leaks if one side disappears`],
        [`buffered channel`, `Queues, bursts, counting semaphore`, `Buffer hides a slow consumer; still need close/ownership rules`],
        [`<code>chan struct{}</code> + close`, `Broadcasting an event / done signal`, `Close only once`],
        [`<code>sync.Mutex</code>`, `Protecting compound state / invariants`, `Not reentrant; forgetting Unlock deadlocks`],
        [`<code>sync.RWMutex</code>`, `Many readers, rare writers`, `Overhead with short critical sections; never write under RLock`],
        [`<code>sync/atomic</code>`, `Single counters, flags, pointer swaps`, `Every access must be atomic; doesn't compose`],
        [`monitor goroutine`, `One owner serializes all access`, `Channel hop per operation`],
        [`<code>context</code>`, `Cancellation, deadlines across API boundaries`, `Always defer cancel(); don't store in structs`],
        [`<code>semaphore.Weighted</code>`, `Limiting concurrency by weight`, `Release exactly what you acquired`]
      ] },
      { title: `Worker pool with context and WaitGroup`, type: `code`, lang: `go`, body: `func run(ctx context.Context, jobs []int, workers int) []int {
    in := make(chan int)
    out := make(chan int, workers)
    var wg sync.WaitGroup

    for w := 0; w < workers; w++ {
        wg.Add(1)                 // Add before go
        go func() {
            defer wg.Done()
            for j := range in {   // exits when in is closed
                select {
                case out <- j * j:
                case <-ctx.Done():
                    return
                }
            }
        }()
    }

    go func() {                   // producer owns and closes in
        defer close(in)
        for _, j := range jobs {
            select {
            case in <- j:
            case <-ctx.Done():
                return
            }
        }
    }()

    go func() { wg.Wait(); close(out) }() // coordinator closes out

    var res []int
    for r := range out {
        res = append(res, r)
    }
    return res
}` },
      { title: `select, timeout and signal patterns`, type: `code`, lang: `go`, body: `// Timeout (buffered so the worker never leaks)
c := make(chan string, 1)
go func() { c <- slowCall() }()
select {
case r := <-c:
    fmt.Println(r)
case <-time.After(time.Second):
    fmt.Println("timeout")
}

// Non-blocking send: drop when full
select {
case queue <- job:
default:
    dropped++
}

// Disable a case by setting its channel to nil
for a != nil || b != nil {
    select {
    case v, ok := <-a:
        if !ok { a = nil; continue }
        use(v)
    case v, ok := <-b:
        if !ok { b = nil; continue }
        use(v)
    }
}

// Cancellation with a cause
ctx, cancel := context.WithCancelCause(context.Background())
cancel(errors.New("quota exceeded"))
<-ctx.Done()
fmt.Println(ctx.Err(), context.Cause(ctx)) // context canceled quota exceeded

// Graceful shutdown on Ctrl+C / SIGTERM
ctx, stop := signal.NotifyContext(context.Background(), os.Interrupt, syscall.SIGTERM)
defer stop()
<-ctx.Done()

// Atomic counter and mutex-protected map
var hits atomic.Int64
hits.Add(1)

var mu sync.Mutex
mu.Lock()
m[k]++
mu.Unlock()` },
      { title: `Scheduler and runtime facts`, type: `list`, items: [
        `<strong>G</strong> = goroutine, <strong>M</strong> = OS thread, <strong>P</strong> = logical processor with a local run queue (256 slots in the runtime). An M needs a P to run Go code.`,
        `<code>GOMAXPROCS</code> = number of Ps (default: logical CPUs). <code>runtime.GOMAXPROCS(0)</code> only reads it.`,
        `A goroutine is <em>executing</em>, <em>runnable</em> or <em>waiting</em>. A blocking syscall detaches the P so another M can run, which means the number of threads can exceed GOMAXPROCS.`,
        `An idle P looks in its local queue, then the global queue (also checked every 61 ticks for fairness), then the netpoller, then <strong>steals half</strong> of a random P's queue.`,
        `Since Go 1.14 goroutines are <strong>asynchronously preempted</strong> (about every 10 ms), so a tight loop can no longer starve the others.`,
        `<code>runtime.NumGoroutine()</code>, <code>runtime.Gosched()</code> (yield) and <code>GODEBUG=schedtrace=1000</code> help you inspect the scheduler.`,
        `"All goroutines are asleep - deadlock!" is detected only when <em>every</em> goroutine is blocked. A single stuck goroutine just leaks.`
      ] }
    ],
    interview: [
      { q: `What is the difference between a goroutine and an OS thread?`, a: `<ul><li><strong>Stack:</strong> a goroutine starts with about 2 KB and grows or shrinks on demand. A thread gets a fixed stack of about 1-8 MB.</li><li><strong>Creation and switching:</strong> creating and switching goroutines happens in user space inside the Go runtime and is cheap. Thread switches go through the kernel and save more state.</li><li><strong>Scheduling:</strong> the OS schedules threads; the Go runtime schedules goroutines onto threads (m:n) and knows when they block on channels, mutexes or network I/O.</li><li><strong>Identity:</strong> goroutines have no exposed ID or handle, and you can't kill one from outside. You ask it to stop (context, done channel).</li></ul><p>The result: hundreds of thousands of goroutines in one process is normal, while that many threads would exhaust memory.</p>`, diff: `easy` },
      { q: `Explain the G-M-P model and what happens when a goroutine makes a blocking system call.`, a: `<p><strong>G</strong> is a goroutine, <strong>M</strong> an OS thread and <strong>P</strong> a logical processor holding a local run queue. There are exactly <code>GOMAXPROCS</code> Ps, and an M must acquire a P to execute Go code, so at most GOMAXPROCS goroutines run in parallel.</p><p>When a G enters a blocking syscall (such as a file read), its M blocks in the kernel. The runtime (the <code>sysmon</code> thread or the syscall exit path) <strong>hands the P off</strong> to another M, creating one if necessary, so the other runnable Gs keep running. When the syscall returns, the G tries to reacquire a P; if none is free it goes to the global queue and the M parks.</p><p>Network I/O works differently: the G is parked on the <strong>netpoller</strong> (epoll/kqueue/IOCP) and no thread blocks, which is why Go servers handle so many connections.</p>`, diff: `hard` },
      { q: `How does work stealing work in the Go scheduler?`, a: `<p>New goroutines go onto the local run queue of the P that created them. When a P runs out of work it: (1) checks its local queue, (2) checks the global queue (also checked every 61 scheduling ticks for fairness, even when local work exists), (3) polls the network, and (4) picks a random victim P and <strong>steals half</strong> of its local queue.</p><p>This avoids a single contended global queue and spreads load from a goroutine that spawns many children to idle Ps. The book adds that Go uses the fork-join model and steals <em>continuations</em>, which keeps behaviour equivalent to plain function calls.</p>`, diff: `med` },
      { q: `What does GOMAXPROCS control, and when would you change it?`, a: `<p>It sets the number of Ps, the maximum number of threads executing Go code simultaneously. It doesn't limit the total number of threads: threads blocked in syscalls or cgo don't count. The default is the number of logical CPUs. <code>runtime.GOMAXPROCS(0)</code> reads it, and <code>runtime.GOMAXPROCS(n)</code> sets it and returns the previous value.</p><p>You rarely change it. Typical reasons: matching a container CPU quota (older Go versions saw all host cores and suffered CFS throttling, hence <code>automaxprocs</code>; Go 1.25 reads cgroup limits), testing behaviour on fewer cores, or reproducing scheduling bugs with <code>GOMAXPROCS=1</code>. A value higher than the number of cores rarely helps, because of context switching.</p>`, diff: `med` },
      { q: `Concurrency vs parallelism: what is the difference?`, a: `<p>Concurrency is about <strong>structure</strong>: decomposing a problem into independently executing pieces that communicate. Parallelism is about <strong>execution</strong>: several things running at the same instant on multiple cores.</p><p>A concurrent program on one core interleaves its goroutines; on many cores the same program can run in parallel with no code change. The book's message: design for concurrency and parallelism follows. More goroutines aren't automatically faster, since scheduling, synchronization and GC overhead can make things slower.</p>`, diff: `easy` },
      { q: `List what happens on send, receive and close for nil, open and closed channels.`, a: `<ul><li><strong>nil channel:</strong> send and receive block forever; close panics.</li><li><strong>open channel:</strong> send blocks until a receiver is ready (unbuffered) or while the buffer is full; receive blocks until a value is available; close wakes every blocked receiver, and any goroutine blocked in a send panics.</li><li><strong>closed channel:</strong> send panics (<code>send on closed channel</code>); receive returns remaining buffered values, then the zero value with <code>ok == false</code> immediately; close panics again.</li></ul><p>Practical consequences: only the sender closes, <code>close</code> is a broadcast, <code>v, ok := &lt;-ch</code> or <code>range</code> detects the end, and a nil channel switches off a <code>select</code> case.</p>`, diff: `med` },
      { q: `What is a data race, how does Go detect it, and is "it works on my machine" enough?`, a: `<p>A data race is two or more goroutines accessing the same memory concurrently, with at least one write and no happens-before ordering between them (no mutex, channel operation or atomic in between). The result is undefined: lost updates, torn reads, broken invariants, and in Go even crashes on maps (<code>concurrent map writes</code>).</p><p><code>-race</code> instruments every memory access and synchronization event (ThreadSanitizer) and reports conflicting accesses with both stack traces. It only reports races that <em>occur during that run</em> and slows the program down by about 2-20x, so run it in tests and CI under realistic concurrency. "Works on my machine" proves nothing: the book's <code>channels.go</code> usually runs fine and occasionally panics with <code>send on closed channel</code>, depending on scheduling.</p>`, diff: `med` },
      { q: `Mutex vs RWMutex vs atomic vs channels: how do you choose?`, a: `<ul><li><strong>atomic</strong> for a single word: counters, flags, swapping a pointer to an immutable config. Fastest, but it doesn't compose across multiple fields.</li><li><strong>Mutex</strong> when several fields must change together or a compound operation (check-then-act, map update) must be atomic. Keep the critical section short and use <code>defer Unlock()</code>.</li><li><strong>RWMutex</strong> when reads greatly outnumber writes and read sections are long enough to amortize its extra cost.</li><li><strong>Channels</strong> when you transfer ownership of data, coordinate stages (pipelines, worker pools) or signal events. A monitor goroutine turns shared state into message passing.</li></ul><p>The book's own advice: channels aren't a silver bullet. Use them when they simplify the design; the concurrent stats app uses only a WaitGroup plus a distinct slice index per goroutine.</p>`, diff: `med` },
      { q: `What happens if wg.Add and wg.Done don't match? Why must Add be called before the go statement?`, a: `<p>If there are more <code>Done()</code> calls than <code>Add()</code>, the counter goes negative and the program panics with <code>sync: negative WaitGroup counter</code>. If there are fewer Dones, <code>Wait()</code> never returns. When every goroutine is blocked, the runtime reports <code>fatal error: all goroutines are asleep - deadlock!</code>; if something else is still running, the program just hangs. As the book notes, the panic doesn't always show up, because <code>main</code> may exit before the extra Done runs.</p><p><code>Add</code> must happen-before <code>Wait</code>. If you call it inside the new goroutine, <code>Wait</code> may observe a counter of 0 and return before that goroutine has started, which is itself a race. Call <code>Add(1)</code> (or <code>Add(n)</code>) in the parent before <code>go</code>, and <code>defer wg.Done()</code> as the first line in the child.</p>`, diff: `easy` },
      { q: `What does this print and why: for i := 0; i &lt; 3; i++ { go func(){ fmt.Print(i) }() }`, a: `<p><strong>Before Go 1.22:</strong> the closure captures the single loop variable <code>i</code>. By the time the goroutines run, the loop has usually finished, so you see <code>333</code> or a mix such as <code>133</code>, and <code>-race</code> reports a race between the loop's write and the goroutines' reads. The book's 0..20 version prints mostly 21s.</p><p><strong>Since Go 1.22</strong> (with <code>go 1.22</code> or later in go.mod) each iteration has its own <code>i</code>, so it prints 0, 1 and 2 in some order. Note that you also need a WaitGroup, or <code>main</code> may exit before any of them print. For older modules, pass the value as an argument (<code>go func(x int){...}(i)</code>) or shadow it with <code>i := i</code>.</p>`, diff: `med` },
      { q: `How does select behave when several cases are ready, and how would you give one channel priority?`, a: `<p>Go picks among the ready cases <strong>uniformly at random</strong>, which prevents starvation. With <code>default</code>, select never blocks. With no ready case and no default, it blocks; <code>select{}</code> blocks forever.</p><p>For priority (for example, handling <code>ctx.Done()</code> before more work), check the high-priority channel first in its own non-blocking select, then do the combined select:</p><p><code>select { case &lt;-ctx.Done(): return; default: }</code> followed by <code>select { case &lt;-ctx.Done(): return; case j := &lt;-jobs: ... }</code>. Even then a job can win the second select, so the worker must tolerate processing one more item.</p>`, diff: `hard` },
      { q: `How does context cancellation propagate, and why must you always call cancel?`, a: `<p>Contexts form a tree. <code>WithCancel</code>, <code>WithTimeout</code> and <code>WithDeadline</code> register the child with its parent. When a context is canceled (explicitly, or because its deadline passed), it closes its <code>Done()</code> channel, sets <code>Err()</code> to <code>Canceled</code> or <code>DeadlineExceeded</code>, and cancels <strong>all its descendants</strong>. Cancellation never flows upward. A child with a later deadline than its parent effectively inherits the parent's earlier deadline.</p><p>Calling <code>cancel()</code> (usually <code>defer cancel()</code>, even on the success path) removes the child from the parent's children map and stops its timer. Without it, the child and its timer stay alive until the parent is canceled, which is a leak in long-lived servers (<code>go vet</code> warns about it). With <code>WithCancelCause</code> you can also attach the reason, which you read with <code>context.Cause(ctx)</code>.</p>`, diff: `hard` },
      { q: `How would you implement a timeout around a slow call without leaking the goroutine?`, a: `<p>A bare <code>select</code> with <code>time.After</code> only stops <em>waiting</em>. If the worker later sends on an unbuffered channel that nobody reads, it blocks forever: a goroutine leak. Two fixes:</p><ol><li>Make the result channel <strong>buffered with capacity 1</strong>, so the late send succeeds and the goroutine exits.</li><li>Better: pass a <code>context.WithTimeout</code> into the work so that it actually <em>stops</em> (database drivers and <code>http.NewRequestWithContext</code> honour it), and select on <code>ctx.Done()</code>.</li></ol><p>Also avoid <code>time.After</code> inside hot loops (before Go 1.23 each un-fired timer stayed alive until it fired). Reuse a <code>time.Timer</code> or one context deadline instead.</p>`, diff: `hard` }
    ],
    labs: [`c08Scheduler`, `c08Channels`, `c08Race`, `c08WorkerPool`, `c08Context`]
  }
);
